Oct5 notes:
Each baseline writes a jsonl to evaluation/outputs/ in the shared format match.py reads:
{"paper_id": "<OpenReview id>", "issues": ["...", ...5], "severities": [1-5, ...]}

Data: ICLR 2024 (neighbor pool) + ICLR 2026 (test set) in evaluation/data/:
cd evaluation && python fetch_reviews.py --years 2024 2026

Non-ML baseline (neighbors retrieved from ICLR 2024: 5,690 papers; ranked by severity; runs on all 13,702 2026 papers):
python baseline/nonMLBaseline.py --test_papers evaluation/data/papers.jsonl --test_year 2026 --train_years 2024

LLM baseline (needs full text, so a random sample of 50 papers):
export OPENREVIEW_USERNAME=... OPENREVIEW_PASSWORD=...
cd evaluation && python fetch_pdfs.py --year 2026 --n 50    # -> data/sample_2026_50.json, data/fulltext/
python baseline/llm_baseline.py --model Qwen/Qwen3-8B --load_in_4bit --max_input_tokens 16000

Evaluate (sentence-similarity matching; precision@5 is the headline metric; ties at the
non-ML top-5 cutoff are scored as the expected value over all tie-breaks):
cd evaluation && python match.py outputs/nonml_iclr2026_severity_train2024.jsonl --matcher embedding --sim-threshold 0.5
cd evaluation && python match.py outputs/llm_Qwen3-8B.jsonl --matcher embedding --sim-threshold 0.5 \
    --paper-ids data/sample_2026_50.json

Oct29 notes (initial ML solution, package mald/):
Data: CASIMIR (official release, huggingface taln-ls2n/CASIMIR). Test = 40 ICLR 2023 papers, val = 12
ICLR 2022 papers (seeded order); pool = ICLR 2020-2022 + NeurIPS 2022; concurrent ICLR 2023 forums unused.

Setup:
pip install -r requirements.txt
export MALD_ROOT=/fast/local/disk/mald      # work root; default ./work (casimir/, build/, out/, cache/, judge/)
Other overrides: MALD_CASIMIR MALD_BUILD MALD_OUT MALD_CACHE MALD_JUDGE MALD_REPORT (default ./report).

LLM servers (llama.cpp, openai-compatible; url per model from MALD_<MODEL>_URL, default local port):
  gemma    8091  google/gemma-4-12B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf         generator (all systems)
  glimmer  8092  meta-models/Muse-Glimmer-30B-GGUF           review parsing for gt, llm matcher; add --reasoning-budget 0
  nemotron 8093  unsloth/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3.5-Bolt-E4B-GGUF   second generator
  qwen     8094  unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF                    optional (QWEN=1)
e.g. llama-server -m <gguf> --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8091 -ngl 999 -c 131072 -np 4 --kv-unified -fa on --jinja --alias gemma
Every reply and token count is cached in MALD_CACHE. With a full cache no server is needed.
MALD_CACHE_ONLY=1 makes a cache miss an error. The agentic configs cluster bge vectors, and tied issues
can come out in another order on another GPU type; configs whose llm meta-review reads that table then
need the gemma server for those papers.

Run everything (resumable; a rerun skips finished papers):
scripts/pipeline.sh            # corpus -> gt -> val sweep -> selection -> test runs -> evaluation -> tables
scripts/report.sh              # tables and figures only -> report/
Single steps:
python -m mald.build                                     # corpus from casimir (downloads it if missing)
python -m mald.gt test 40                                # gt from the real reviews
python -m mald.run test 40 m m '{"theta":0.3,"w":0,"llm_meta":false}'
python -m mald.evaluate test judge m b1 b0 b1old         # judge: also score with the llm matcher
Systems: b0 = non-ML baseline, b1old = ML baseline, b1 = ours (one prompt), m = ours (agentic).

LLM judge (secondary check, 12 test papers):
MALD_JUDGE=$MALD_ROOT/judge python -m mald.judge_prep 12 m b1 b0     # bundles + spec in the judge dir
then give each judge the spec in the judge dir and two papers, and let it write <judge dir>/out/<forum>.json;
MALD_JUDGE=<dir>[:<dir>] python -m mald.judge_agg > report/judge_tables.tex
